{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalInequivalence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalInequivalence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.QJucysMurphyEigen","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.MatRepEquiv","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.GenericParameterReal","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Roots"],"declarations":25,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.coshSum_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.coshSum_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":505,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L505-L534","doc":"**The bar-symmetrised content sum is transpose-invariant.**  Transposing a Young diagram negates\nevery content, and `q^{2c} + q^{−2c}` is even in `c`, so the sum is unchanged — at **every** `q`.\n\nConsequence: `barSymJMSum` acts by the *same* scalar on `ρ_λ` and `ρ_{λ'}`, so the cosh criterion is\nvacuous on every transpose pair.  That is why the unconditional argument goes through the whole\npower-sum family and the content *counts* rather than through this single scalar.\n\nMeasured in exact rational arithmetic, the collapse is total: at `n = 7` the 15 partitions give only\n8 distinct values of the cosh sum, and every collision is exactly a transpose pair.\n\nThis also sharpens what `cellContentCoshSum_eq_bracket` records (that the bar-even brackets are not\nindependent spectral degrees of freedom): the invariant is not merely dependent on the others, it is\nstrictly *coarser than the partition itself*. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.youngDiagram_eq_of_contentPowSum_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.youngDiagram_eq_of_contentPowSum_eq","kind":"theorem","line":479,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L479-L495","doc":"**Equal content power sums force equal diagrams.**  The scalar-level form of\n`seminormalRep_not_matRepEquiv_of_ne`, with no representation in the statement at all: if the\ncentral Murphy power sums take the same value on two shapes of the same size, the shapes coincide.\n\nThis is the form a *product-map* argument needs.  `seminormalRep_not_matRepEquiv_of_ne` answers\n\"are these two blocks isomorphic\"; assembling `⊕_λ ρ_λ` instead needs \"do the block scalars\nseparate the index set\", which is this. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep_not_matRepEquiv_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep_not_matRepEquiv_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":456,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L456-L477","doc":"**Pairwise inequivalence of the `q`-deformed seminormal representations — unconditional.**\n\nDistinct shapes of the same size carry inequivalent representations.  No hypothesis on the shapes\nbeyond `μ ≠ ν`, and none on `q` beyond the genericity `seminormalRep` already requires.\n\nThe chain, all sorry-free: an equivalence makes `Σ_k L_k^m` act by the same scalar for every `m`\n(`contentPowSum_eq_of_matRepEquiv`); that family of coincidences is a polynomial identity in\ndisguise, because `q ^ 2 ≠ 1` makes the evaluation points `q^2, q^4, …` distinct\n(`contentCount_eq_of_matRepEquiv`); and a Young diagram is determined by its content multiset,\nbecause its diagonals are downward-closed (`youngDiagram_eq_of_contentCount_eq`).\n\nTogether with irreducibility this is what makes `{ρ_λ : λ ⊢ n}` the *list* of irreducibles of\n`H_n(q)` rather than merely a supply of them — the second half of SW-5 (bean `qou-jfr8`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.youngDiagram_eq_of_contentCount_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.youngDiagram_eq_of_contentCount_eq","kind":"theorem","line":444,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L444-L451","doc":"**A Young diagram is determined by its content multiset.**  Equal content counts give equal\ndiagonal lengths, and `mem_iff_diagIndex_lt` turns those into the same membership predicate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.mem_iff_diagIndex_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.mem_iff_diagIndex_lt","kind":"theorem","line":418,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L418-L442","doc":"**Membership is decided by the diagonal length.**  A cell lies in `μ` exactly when its\ndiagonal index is below the number of cells of its content. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.diagIndex_image_downward","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.diagIndex_image_downward","kind":"theorem","line":396,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L396-L416","doc":"**Diagonals are downward-closed.**  Stepping one cell up-and-left along a diagonal stays in\nthe diagram (up-left closure), keeps the content, and drops the diagonal index by one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.mem_diagOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.mem_diagOf","kind":"theorem","line":391,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L391-L394","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.diagOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.diagOf","kind":"def","line":385,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L385-L389","doc":"The `d`-diagonal of `μ` — the cells of content `d`.  Its cardinality is the multiplicity of\n`d` in the content multiset. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.cell_eq_of_content_of_diagIndex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.cell_eq_of_content_of_diagIndex","kind":"theorem","line":376,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L376-L383","doc":"A cell is determined by its content together with its diagonal index. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.diagIndex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.diagIndex","kind":"def","line":373,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L373-L374","doc":"The position of a cell along its own diagonal, counted from the boundary of the quadrant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.mem_iff_lt_card_of_downward","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.mem_iff_lt_card_of_downward","kind":"theorem","line":350,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L350-L371","doc":"A downward-closed finite set of naturals is an initial segment. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.contentCount_eq_of_matRepEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.contentCount_eq_of_matRepEquiv","kind":"theorem","line":326,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L326-L335","doc":"**An equivalence forces equal content counts.**  The original form of the previous theorem,\nnow a corollary of it: `MatRepEquiv` is only ever used to supply the power-sum equality. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.contentCount_eq_of_contentPowSum_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.contentCount_eq_of_contentPowSum_eq","kind":"theorem","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L268-L324","doc":"**An equivalence forces the two shapes to have the same content multiset.**\n\nThe unconditional handle, assembled: `contentPowSum_eq_of_matRepEquiv` gives equality of the\ncontent power sums at *every* `m`; shifting the contents into `ℕ` turns those into honest\npolynomial evaluations at `t = q ^ 2`, `t ^ 2`, `t ^ 3`, …; and `card_filter_eq_of_powSum_eq`\nconverts infinitely many coincidences into equality of coefficients — that is, of the number of\ncells of each content.\n\nThe genericity hypothesis is doing a *second* job here.  Elsewhere in this arc `q ^ 2 ≠ 1` is\nused for injectivity of `c ↦ q ^ (2c)`; here it is what makes the evaluation points\n`q ^ 2, q ^ 4, …` pairwise distinct, so that the difference polynomial has infinitely many roots.\n\nWhat remains between this and `μ = ν` is the classical fact that a Young diagram is determined by\nits content multiset — stated in the module header as the one open step, and deliberately not\nassumed here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.pow_shiftedContent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.pow_shiftedContent","kind":"theorem","line":255,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L255-L265","doc":"`t ^ shiftedContent` factors as `q ^ (2·content)` times the shift, with `t = q ^ 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.shiftedContent_cast","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.shiftedContent_cast","kind":"theorem","line":248,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L248-L253","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.shiftedContent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.shiftedContent","kind":"def","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L245-L246","doc":"The content of a cell, shifted into `ℕ` by the row bound. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.neg_le_cellContent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.neg_le_cellContent","kind":"theorem","line":236,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L236-L243","doc":"Contents are bounded below by `-n` when the diagram has `n + 1` cells. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.row_lt_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.row_lt_card","kind":"theorem","line":220,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L220-L234","doc":"**A cell's row index is below the cell count.**  The cells `(0,j), …, (i,j)` all lie in `μ`\nby up-left closure and are distinct, so there are at least `i + 1` of them.  This is what bounds\nthe contents from below and lets them be shifted into `ℕ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.card_filter_eq_of_powSum_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.card_filter_eq_of_powSum_eq","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L193-L215","doc":"**Equal power sums at every `m` force equal exponent multisets.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.coeff_sum_X_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.coeff_sum_X_pow","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L178-L191","doc":"The `d`-th coefficient of `∑_{i ∈ s} X ^ (f i)` counts the `i` with `f i = d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.poly_eq_zero_of_eval_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.poly_eq_zero_of_eval_pow","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L172-L176","doc":"A polynomial vanishing at every `t ^ m` is the zero polynomial. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.pow_right_injective_of_pos_of_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.pow_right_injective_of_pos_of_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L161-L170","doc":"`m ↦ t ^ m` is injective for a non-root-of-unity `t`.\n\nKept as a named local so the call sites below read unchanged; the content is entirely\n`GenericParameter.pow_left_injective`, which holds over **any** field.  Until 2026-09-03\nthis took `0 < t` and `t ≠ 1` and went through `Real.log_pow`, which fused \"not a root of\nunity\" with the `ℝ`-only proof of it.  Bean `qou-j377`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.contentPowSum_eq_of_matRepEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.contentPowSum_eq_of_matRepEquiv","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L142-L150","doc":"**An equivalence forces every content power sum to agree.**  The unconditional handle on\npairwise inequivalence: not one number, but a whole family indexed by `m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep_jmPowSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep_jmPowSum","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L114-L140","doc":"**The `m`-th Murphy power sum acts by the content power sum.**  Generalises\n`seminormalRep_barSymJMSum` from the bar-symmetrised `m = ±1` combination to every `m`, by the\nsame two ingredients: Ram 4.1 makes each `ρ(L_k)` diagonal, and `sum_cellOf_range` makes the\ndiagonal constant because summing over `k` sweeps every cell exactly once. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep_not_matRepEquiv_of_contentCoshSum_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormalRep_not_matRepEquiv_of_contentCoshSum_ne","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/SeminormalInequivalence.lean#L78-L95","doc":"**Shapes with different content-cosh sums carry inequivalent representations.**\n\n`Θ = barSymJMSum` is central, and `seminormalRep_barSymJMSum` evaluates it to the scalar\n`Σ_{c ∈ μ} (q^{2c} + q^{−2c})`.  By `MatRepEquiv.scalar_eq` an equivalence would force the two\nsums to agree.\n\n**Read the hypothesis, not the name.**  This separates exactly the pairs of shapes whose sums\ndiffer *at this `q`*.  It is not `μ ≠ ν → ρ_μ ≇ ρ_ν`; see the module docstring for why one\nscalar cannot deliver that, and what does. ","signatureChunk":0}]}